---
import Guide from "../../../layouts/Guide.astro"
import { Callout, CalloutContent } from "opui-css/astro"
import Code from "../../../components/Code.astro"
import CodeGroup from "../../../components/CodeGroup.astro"
import DocLink from "../../../components/DocLink.astro"
import Example from "../../../components/Example/index"
import { pickAndChooseCss } from "../../../utils/getting-started"
---

<Guide overline="Guide">
  <Fragment slot="title">Getting started</Fragment>
  <Fragment slot="preamble">
    Design system. Components. Modern HTML & CSS.
  </Fragment>

  <div class="ui-not-rich-text">
    <Callout severity="neutral">
      <CalloutContent>
        <p>
          Open Props UI is <u>just CSS</u>. You can copy any <DocLink
            class="ui-link"
            href="/components">component</DocLink
          > CSS straight from the docs - no install required. Each component page
          has its CSS under Installation, with the files it depends on and a button
          that copies them all. It reads tokens from Open Props, <code
            >palette.css</code
          > and <code>theme.css</code>, so those need to be on the page too,
          after <code>layers.css</code> sets the layer order. <code
            >normalize.css</code
          > adds the focus ring and
          <code>utils.css</code> adds <code>.ui-sr-only</code> and the motion classes.
          The CDN link below has all of it in one file.
        </p>
      </CalloutContent>
    </Callout>
  </div>

  <section>
    <h2 id="cdn">Install via CDN</h2>
    <p>
      The quickest way to get going. One file with Open Props, the theme, the
      reset, every component and the utility classes, in the right cascade
      layers.
    </p>
    <Code
      code={`<link rel="stylesheet" href="https://cdn.jsdelivr.net/npm/opui-css@6/dist/opui.css" />`}
      lang="html"
    />
    <p>
      <code>@6</code> loads the newest 6.x release, so a new major version never changes
      your site. Pin an exact version, such as <code>@6.0.0</code>, for full
      control.
    </p>
  </section>

  <section>
    <h2 id="installation">Install via NPM</h2>
    <p>
      For when you have a bundler that resolves CSS <code>@import</code>s from
      <code>node_modules</code>, like Vite or webpack. No bundler? Use the CDN
      or install manually.
    </p>

    <CodeGroup
      tabs={[
        {
          title: "pnpm",
          code: "pnpm add opui-css open-props",
          lang: "sh",
        },
        {
          title: "npm",
          code: "npm install opui-css open-props",
          lang: "sh",
        },
      ]}
    />

    <h3 id="css">Import the CSS</h3>
    <p>Either import everything in your main CSS file:</p>
    <Code code={`@import "opui-css/css/imports.css";`} lang="css" />

    <p>Or pick and choose the parts you want to include:</p>
    <Code code={pickAndChooseCss} lang="css" />
    <p>
      Import <code>layers.css</code> first and <code>utils.css</code> last, it holds
      <code>.ui-sr-only</code> and the motion classes. Add <code
        >choice.css</code
      > with <code>checkbox.css</code>, <code>radio.css</code> or <code
        >switch.css</code
      >. Import <code>opui-css/css/components.css</code> instead of single component
      files to get every component.
    </p>
  </section>

  <section>
    <h2 id="manual-setup">Install manually</h2>
    <p>
      The most flexible way to use OPUI, and it works without a build step.
      Download these files from the <a
        class="ui-link"
        target="_blank"
        href="https://github.com/felix-bohlin/ui/tree/main/packages/opui"
        ><code>packages/opui/</code> folder on GitHub</a
      > and drop them into your project.
    </p>

    <Code
      code={`opui
├─ core
│  ├─ normalize.css
│  ├─ palette.css
│  └─ utils.css
├─ css
│  ├─ components
│  │  ├─ accordion.css
│  │  └─ ...
│  ├─ js
│  │  └─ checkbox.js
│  ├─ components.css
│  ├─ layers.css
│  └─ theme.css
└─ op.css`}
      lang="text"
    />

    <p>
      <code>op.css</code> is Open Props, pre-bundled in the <code
        >openprops</code
      > layer. It isn't in the repo, so grab it from <a
        class="ui-link"
        target="_blank"
        href="https://cdn.jsdelivr.net/npm/opui-css@6/dist/op.css">the CDN</a
      > (or <code>dist/op.css</code> in the npm package). The package's <code
        >open-props.css</code
      > imports Open Props from <code>node_modules</code>, which only works with
      a bundler.
    </p>

    <p>
      Put it all together something like this in your main CSS. If you have your
      files in a different folder structure you'd of course need to change the
      paths.
    </p>
    <Code
      code={`@import "./opui/css/layers.css";

@import "./opui/op.css";
@import "./opui/core/palette.css";
@import "./opui/css/theme.css";
@import "./opui/core/normalize.css";
@import "./opui/css/components.css";
@import "./opui/core/utils.css";`}
      lang="css"
    />
  </section>

  <section>
    <h2 id="usage">Your first component</h2>
    <p>
      With the CSS on the page, a component is a class on the right element. The
      modifiers are classes too.
    </p>

    <Example centered>
      <button class="ui-button ui-filled ui-primary" type="button">
        Click me
      </button>
    </Example>

    <p>
      A few components need a bit of JavaScript in plain HTML, such as
      indeterminate checkboxes in <code>css/js/checkbox.js</code>. Import them
      in a <code>&lt;script type="module"&gt;</code>, from <code
        >https://cdn.jsdelivr.net/npm/opui-css@6/css/js/</code
      > without a bundler. <DocLink href="/guide/concepts#javascript"
        >Concepts</DocLink
      > shows the imports and explains the class names, the cascade layers and what
      needs JavaScript. Then browse the <DocLink href="/components"
        >components</DocLink
      >. <DocLink href="/guide/theming">Theming</DocLink> covers colors, density,
      motion and contrast.
    </p>
  </section>
</Guide>
